<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Istituto per la Ricostruzione Industriale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as IRI, Institute for Industrial Reconstruction</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Istituto per la Ricostruzione Industriale (IRI) was an Italian state holding company founded in 1933 to rescue banks and industrial firms ruined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. It took controlling stakes in major banks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, shipbuilding and engineering, making the Italian state one of the largest industrial owners in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. IRI remained central to the post-war economic miracle and was dissolved in 2002.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istituto-per-la-ricostruzione-industriale.jpg" alt="Istituto per la Ricostruzione Industriale" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IRI S.p.A. - Vettorializzato da: Leonardo Compagnoni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_IRI.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
